# TOML has no null. The exporter used to render one as an empty string, so
# a null and a genuine "" came out as two identical values that no reader
# could tell apart, and neither survived a round trip. It must report
# instead of conflating them (eu-1tkk.7.28).
#
# The null arrives from imported JSON rather than as a literal, both
# because that is how nullable data actually reaches an export and because
# the lookup carries a source location for the diagnostic to point at.
#
# Kept at the document root rather than inside a block literal: under
# HeapSyn a value nested in a block literal loses its primary label
# entirely, so the block form would assert a location the bytecode engine
# produces and HeapSyn does not (eu-1tkk.7.26). Same reason as fixture 194.
` { target: :main format: :toml import: "data=testdata/nullable.json" }
main: data.absent
